{
  "id": "Compound::DB01280 - Disease::DOID:2531",
  "verbalization": "The visible evidence suggests that Nelarabine may be associated with Hematologic Cancer through its inclusion in the class of Nucleic Acid Synthesis Inhibitors, which includes Cytarabine and Pentostatin. Both Cytarabine and Pentostatin are known to inhibit nucleic acid synthesis, a mechanism that is commonly used in the treatment of hematologic cancers. This pathway implies that Nelarabine, as an inhibitor of nucleic acid synthesis, may contribute to the treatment of Hematologic Cancer through similar mechanisms. Additionally, the shared lowest common ancestors between Nelarabine and these compounds—Antimetabolite and N-Glycosyl Compound—suggest a common biological pathway that may underlie their therapeutic effects.\n\nThe evidence also indicates that Nelarabine may contribute to the treatment of Hematologic Cancer through a different mechanism, namely, by causing Non-cardiac Chest Pain, which is then treated by Nilotinib. Nilotinib, in turn, is used to treat Hematologic Cancer, suggesting a potential indirect relationship between Nelarabine and Hematologic Cancer through the Nilotinib pathway. However, the strength of this connection is not as strong as the direct relationship observed through the Nucleic Acid Synthesis Inhibitors class. The integration of these pathways highlights the complexity of drug interactions and the importance of considering multiple mechanisms when evaluating the potential for drug repositioning.",
